Why AGPL-3.0 rather than MIT or Apache-2.0

The AGPL closes the network loophole in the GPL: anyone who modifies this
software and makes it available to users over a network must offer those users
the corresponding source. For a hosted ERP that is the whole point — it keeps
the project open while preventing a third party from taking it closed and
running it as a competing service.

Note this obliges Vritti AI Platforms too. As the copyright holder, Vritti may
additionally offer the software under separate commercial terms; that is a
decision for the company, not a right the AGPL grants to anyone else.

Keeping that option open is why outside contributions require a Contributor
Licence Agreement — see CONTRIBUTING.md and CLA.md. Without one, contributed
code arrives under the AGPL and nothing more, and no later commercial licence
can cover it without the written permission of every contributor who wrote it.

